- Fully comply with **[COMM-GRPC-PROTOBUF-REQ]** by using idiomatic Protobuf definitions optimized for gRPC.
- Are designed to enable low-latency serialization/deserialization and efficient data transfer to meet the **[LATENCY-MAX-150.75MS]** constraint.
- Map cleanly to Apache Cassandra’s wide-column data model, using appropriate data types and partition keys to support fast queries and writes.
- Include versioning and extension options to allow smooth schema evolution without breaking downstream consumers.
- Support the branching tree communication pattern by incorporating fields for coordination, tracing, and synchronization metadata.

### Initial Protobuf Schema Design Outline

```proto
syntax = "proto3";

package financial.streaming;

import "google/protobuf/timestamp.proto";

// Transaction message representing a financial transaction event
message Transaction {
  string transaction_id = 1;               // Unique transaction identifier
  string account_id = 2;                   // Account involved in the transaction
  double amount = 3;                       // Transaction amount
  string currency = 4;                     // ISO 4217 currency code
  google.protobuf.Timestamp event_time = 5; // Event occurrence timestamp
  TransactionType type = 6;                // Enum for transaction type
  string merchant_id = 7;                  // Optional merchant identifier
  repeated string tags = 8;                // Optional tags for categorization
  map<string, string> metadata = 9;       // Additional extensible metadata

  // Coordination metadata for branching tree communication
  string upstream_coordinator_id = 10;
  string trace_id = 11;
}

// Enum for transaction types
enum TransactionType {
  UNKNOWN = 0;
  PURCHASE = 1;
  REFUND = 2;
  TRANSFER = 3;
}

// Control message for coordination and synchronization
message ControlMessage {
  string coordinator_id = 1;
  ControlType control_type = 2;
  google.protobuf.Timestamp timestamp = 3;
  string description = 4;
  map<string, string> parameters = 5;
}

enum ControlType {
  HEARTBEAT = 0;
  SYNC = 1;
  SHUTDOWN = 2;
  CONFIG_UPDATE = 3;
}

// Streaming service interface
service TransactionStreamService {
  // Stream of incoming transactions from upstream coordinator to agents
  rpc StreamTransactions(stream Transaction) returns (StreamAck);
  // Bi-directional control message stream for synchronization
  rpc ControlChannel(stream ControlMessage) returns (stream ControlAck);
}

message StreamAck {
  string ack_id = 1;
  bool success = 2;
  string message = 3;
}

message ControlAck {
  string ack_id = 1;
  bool success = 2;
  string message = 3;
}
```

### Notes on Schema and Design Decisions:
- Use of `google.protobuf.Timestamp` for precise event timing to assist latency measurement and ordering.
- Inclusion of coordination fields (`upstream_coordinator_id`, `trace_id`) to support synchronization and tracing in the branching tree.
- Use of maps and repeated fields to allow extensibility without breaking backward compatibility.
- Separate control messages stream to handle coordination commands asynchronously and reduce coupling.
- Enums for well-defined transaction and control types improve schema clarity.
- Designed for Cassandra storage:
  - `transaction_id` as a unique identifier for fast lookups.
  - `account_id`, `event_time`, and `type` can be used as partition and clustering keys downstream.
  - Metadata stored as map to support flexible attributes.

